WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.859
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:02.453
- Welcome back, everybody!

00:00:02.453 --> 00:00:05.220
Our next guest is a social
media star, bringing awareness

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.703
to a subject most people
find extremely challenging.

00:00:07.703 --> 00:00:10.860
She is a hospice nurse who
not only brings comfort

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.350
to her patients in their
final chapter of life,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.300
she's bringing hope and
understanding to her

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.550
2 million plus followers on TikTok.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.383
Check out what I mean.

00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:21.090
- I had this younger
patient, like late forties,

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.520
who was passing of cancer
and she was never married.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.690
I showed up for a visit one day

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.520
and she's like, "I think I'm going crazy."

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.050
And of course I'm like,
"Why do you think that?"

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.850
And she says, "This guy I went to college

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.570
with is standing at the end of my bed."

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.260
And I said, "Did he pass?"

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.890
She goes, "I don't think so.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.820
Last I saw him was our college graduation.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.690
I took a job across the
country and we broke up."

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.770
So I'm intrigued. And I
said, "Can we Google him?"

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.762
So we did. And I found his obituary.

00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:46.181
I'm reading it and I said,

00:00:46.181 --> 00:00:48.300
"Doesn't look like he was married."

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.920
And I got a smile out of her.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.042
So I firmly believe they're together now.

00:00:52.042 --> 00:00:53.490
(audience exclaims)

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.370
- Oh, she just put out
her first book, y'all.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.010
It's a New York Times bestseller
called "The In-Between"

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.500
Unforgettable Encounters
During Life's Final Moments.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.090
Everybody, please give a warm welcome

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.966
to nurse Hadley Vlahos.

00:01:04.966 --> 00:01:09.049
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.117
Oh my God. She walked out and I was like,

00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:23.250
"I own this dress."

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.320
That was amazing. I love this dress.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.460
It looks so great on you.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.853
Thank you for being here.
- Thank you.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.220
- Thank you for having
me. Yes, this is crazy!

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.053
- Abso-

00:01:30.053 --> 00:01:31.770
Oh my God, I love your accent so much.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.870
Okay, so wait, do you
experience like that video,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.670
do you experience like
sightings like that?

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:36.990
Do people experience that often?

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.340
- Yeah, it's common.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.140
And when I first got into hospice

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.037
and I started seeing it, I was like,

00:01:42.037 --> 00:01:44.580
"Okay, is this a religious
thing? Is this certain people?"

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.410
And I was really surprised
to start talking to my other

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.760
coworkers who have been doing it forever.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.946
And they're like, "No, it
doesn't matter their background.

00:01:49.946 --> 00:01:51.958
It doesn't matter how they were raised."

00:01:51.958 --> 00:01:54.249
This happens to so many different people

00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:57.360
and it's very peaceful,
which is what I love.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.430
Like people are coming to
get them from the other side

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.590
and it brings them a lot
of peace. It's beautiful.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.398
- Yeah. At such a not, so great time.

00:02:05.398 --> 00:02:06.870
You know what I'm saying?
- Yes.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.688
- It's a very hard situation.

00:02:08.688 --> 00:02:11.385
So to find beauty in that is wonderful.

00:02:11.385 --> 00:02:13.080
It's important for you, right?

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.516
To share this experience
with everybody, right?

00:02:14.516 --> 00:02:18.810
- It is, I didn't expect it
to be so healing in a way.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.078
It's hard whenever you have
patients who pass with regrets.

00:02:22.078 --> 00:02:24.300
And so whenever I share

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.730
and people say, "Oh,
I'm learning from this,"

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.469
or "I am gaining something from this,"

00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:31.980
it makes me feel like my
patients are living on in a way.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.930
- Yeah. And still teaching everybody.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:34.800
What a beautiful thing.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.420
Like lessons you're learning as well.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:37.253
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:37.253 --> 00:02:38.251
- [Kelly] Like while sitting there

00:02:38.251 --> 00:02:39.084
and all their advice.
- Yeah.

00:02:39.084 --> 00:02:41.550
- What's like one thing that
you love that they've told you,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.740
that you've really taken
to heart? Do you remember?

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.795
- Yeah. There is one patient
who, called Elizabeth,

00:02:46.795 --> 00:02:50.940
and she was much younger
whenever to be on hospice.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.320
She was in her forties.
- Yeah.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.600
- And I had an eating disorder at the time

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.950
that no one knew about.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.349
And she had a disease,
she had lung cancer,

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:00.060
never smoked, picture of health.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.230
Don't know why she had it,

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.622
we still don't know why she had it.

00:03:02.622 --> 00:03:04.617
She had such a positive outlook.

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.104
And I remember sitting
there one day just charting

00:03:07.104 --> 00:03:09.810
and she said, "Can I give you some advice?

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.673
'Cause I see a lot of myself in you."

00:03:11.673 --> 00:03:14.610
And I said, "Yeah, of
course." Put my computer away.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.274
And she said, "I think I spent too much

00:03:16.274 --> 00:03:17.744
of my life on the treadmill."

00:03:17.744 --> 00:03:21.450
Said, "I really wish I would've
spent more time at the beach

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.400
without worrying what
my stomach looked like

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.374
and more time with my friends at dinners.

00:03:25.374 --> 00:03:27.997
And I really wish I would've
just eaten the cake."

00:03:27.997 --> 00:03:30.300
And she hasn't-

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.230
- Metaphorically and physically.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.280
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.320
And she had no idea how
much that meant to me.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.230
I actually left and went and ate some cake

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.581
that night at a Christmas party.

00:03:38.581 --> 00:03:40.508
- Yeah. Like she told me to eat the cake.

00:03:40.508 --> 00:03:41.875
(audience applauds)

00:03:41.875 --> 00:03:42.870
- Yeah, yeah,
- Yeah.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.350
So the book is now becoming
a TV series, which is huge!

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.183
Congratulations on that.

00:03:47.183 --> 00:03:48.661
- Thank you. Thank you.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:49.921
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:03:49.921 --> 00:03:51.210
Yeah, I'm excited for it.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.990
I'm excited for it to reach
more people in different ways.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:56.700
- Yeah. And to kind of
talk about something

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.350
that people don't really
like to talk about.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.183
You know what I'm saying?

00:03:59.183 --> 00:04:00.660
Because it can be sad for a lot of people.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.840
And even like talking with my children

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.120
about death and grief and
all that kind of stuff

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.200
'cause they're young and they're learning

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.250
about stuff like that.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.310
I like to try and talk about
it in a more positive manner

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.680
of like, "Well, let's worry
about living and not dying."

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.269
Like let's worry about this part.

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:16.102
- Yeah.

00:04:16.102 --> 00:04:17.550
- And I don't know, I like that it'll kind

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.530
of be in our brains a little bit more too.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:21.480
And that direct impact
of the whole situation

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.220
and not being so scary for everybody.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.210
- I think that's so important.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.100
Whenever you talk about
it, takes the fear away.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.000
- Yeah.
- And I think that's really

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.290
important for a lot of people.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.700
- Everything I wanted
to say was everything

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.391
you just said when you
take the fear out of it.

00:04:32.391 --> 00:04:34.127
The weight of it. That was you.

00:04:34.127 --> 00:04:35.247
Yeah, well said.

00:04:35.247 --> 00:04:37.743
She writes books. (laughter)

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:39.116
Hadley's pretty good at it.

00:04:39.116 --> 00:04:41.640
Hadley's book is called "The In-Between"

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.310
Unforgettable Encounters
During Life's Final Moments.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.639
And everyone in the audience
is going home with a copy!

00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:48.480
(audience cheers)

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.460
Hadley's dedicated her life to looking

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.770
after people during the
final days of their lives.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.637
For her, it's a passion and a career,

00:04:54.637 --> 00:04:58.140
but there are upward of 65 million people

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.746
across the country caring
for their own loved ones

00:05:00.746 --> 00:05:03.826
who are elderly, disabled,
or chronically ill.

00:05:03.826 --> 00:05:05.156
They don't get paid.

00:05:05.156 --> 00:05:08.479
They have to give up all their free time.

00:05:08.479 --> 00:05:11.055
They make huge sacrifices, our next,

00:05:11.055 --> 00:05:12.974
and it's life altering.

00:05:12.974 --> 00:05:15.217
And our next guest knows
that experience well.

00:05:15.217 --> 00:05:17.845
And now she's stepping
up for fellow caregivers

00:05:17.845 --> 00:05:20.670
by giving them love and
much deserved pampering.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.813
So that makes her truly a Rad Human.

00:05:22.813 --> 00:05:25.396
(upbeat music)

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.989
(audience applauds)

00:05:28.989 --> 00:05:30.390
From Caregiver Safe Place,

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.409
let's welcome Carletta everybody.

00:05:32.409 --> 00:05:35.790
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.663
- Thank you.

00:05:36.663 --> 00:05:38.220
- So y'all have met?

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.692
Have y'all met before Hadley?

00:05:39.692 --> 00:05:40.560
- Yes, just now.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:41.600
- Oh, before, backstage.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.626
Okay. They go way back! (laughter)

00:05:44.626 --> 00:05:46.888
So what is your experience as caregiver?

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:49.476
- My experience as a caregiver is,

00:05:49.476 --> 00:05:51.998
is just like anyone else's.

00:05:51.998 --> 00:05:55.820
We just get thrown into this...

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.740
situation,
- Situation?

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.630
- where you're a caregiver

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:05.280
and you don't know what
you're gonna have to do.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.930
It's not like a job where they teach you

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:10.498
or treat you as though you're gonna know

00:06:10.498 --> 00:06:12.265
what your job description is.

00:06:12.265 --> 00:06:14.370
Your job description is basically

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.050
to love on your loved ones.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.703
- Yeah.
- And my mom,

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.420
she was my everything.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.519
So I had to take on that
responsibility of loving her

00:06:24.519 --> 00:06:26.143
until her last days.

00:06:26.143 --> 00:06:27.780
- Yeah. Woo!

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.440
So Hadley, what was it
like to see, I mean,

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:34.002
I'm sure you've seen caregivers
just burn out, right?

00:06:34.002 --> 00:06:35.070
That has to be hard.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.000
- It's extremely hard.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.100
- I mean, physically, emotionally,
mentally, all the way.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.310
- It is and y'all are the unsung heroes.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.890
People don't even realize
what people are going through

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.290
every day trying to care
for their loved one.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:47.760
It's extremely difficult.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:48.593
- [Carletta] Thank you.

00:06:48.593 --> 00:06:49.560
- Yeah. Especially if
they can't afford care

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:50.827
and it's just like one of
those things where it's like,

00:06:50.827 --> 00:06:52.230
"I don't know what I'm doing."

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:53.970
- Yeah.
- Like I'm not qualified,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.800
but you're thrown into this situation.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.060
- You, you learn.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.205
You figure it out really fast.

00:06:58.205 --> 00:07:01.740
- Yeah. Well, Carletta, you
created help for caregivers.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.449
So how did this come about?

00:07:03.449 --> 00:07:07.380
- I was in my bed after
taking care of my mom

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.964
and with caregiving,
it plays a mental role

00:07:10.964 --> 00:07:13.950
where you think that you're going crazy.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.745
You don't realize that you're just,

00:07:15.745 --> 00:07:18.360
you're grieving from the moment you start

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.541
taking care of them to the end.

00:07:20.541 --> 00:07:25.074
So I sat in my bed and I said,
"If I'm feeling this way,

00:07:25.074 --> 00:07:28.530
I know it's so many
other caregivers around

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:32.021
that's feeling the same
anxiety that I feel."

00:07:32.021 --> 00:07:33.690
- Yeah.
- And sometimes I thought

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:36.414
I was crazy because I would
be mad at her thinking

00:07:36.414 --> 00:07:39.960
that she was doing it on purpose
and then I would be happy

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.670
because she would be happy.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.074
But then, the moment hits that

00:07:44.074 --> 00:07:47.614
you need to get it together.

00:07:47.614 --> 00:07:50.204
You have to stay strong for that person.

00:07:50.204 --> 00:07:53.666
So with me, I started the foundation

00:07:53.666 --> 00:07:57.990
because I said if I was going
through this, I know it had

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.240
to be someone else that
was going through this

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.836
that was not able to get
the help that they needed.

00:08:02.836 --> 00:08:06.878
So I started the foundation
so that I could help

00:08:06.878 --> 00:08:11.712
as many caregivers as I
can around the world so

00:08:11.712 --> 00:08:15.120
that no one will feel the same stress

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.460
and anxiety that I felt as a caregiver.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.444
- Yeah. And just to talk to
someone that's going through it.

00:08:20.444 --> 00:08:24.150
You know what I'm saying?
All those feelings are valid.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.620
- Yeah.
- How many people have you

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:26.986
been able to help so far?

00:08:26.986 --> 00:08:29.750
- So far it's been 35 to 40 people.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.784
- Wow.
- In which I,

00:08:31.784 --> 00:08:36.351
November I gave out about

00:08:36.351 --> 00:08:40.650
$1,700 gift cards.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.530
Just everybody that was
submitted to the website.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.450
- Yeah.
- I actually mailed those out

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.164
as a surprise for
November Caregiver Month.

00:08:48.164 --> 00:08:53.164
And so normally all the
other ones, I will randomly,

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.738
if I see you at the airport
and I see you have a loved one

00:08:55.738 --> 00:08:57.930
and I feel like you're a family caregiver,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.670
I'll give you a hundred dollar gift card

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:03.648
or a hundred dollars or
whichever one actually works.

00:09:03.648 --> 00:09:08.250
Others, I actually fly
across wherever, to Chicago.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.069
I've gone to Chicago to Tampa, to

00:09:10.069 --> 00:09:13.740
several different places so far, Orlando.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.790
- Yeah.
- And I've given-

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.040
- How do you do that?

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.628
How do you keep that up financially?

00:09:18.628 --> 00:09:21.450
- I do it all out of my
pocket as of right now.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:22.510
- What?
- Yeah.

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.770
I'm a hairstylist. So what I do is I had

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.840
to stay at work from morning to night

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.540
because I needed to make enough
money so I can fly to Tampa

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.570
and stay there for a
week to surprise this man

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.130
that was taking care of his wife.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.810
- Yeah.
- And so I did that

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.510
and we raised $200 and something
thousand dollars for him.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:40.958
- Wow!

00:09:40.958 --> 00:09:43.688
(audience applauds)

00:09:43.688 --> 00:09:45.347
What? That's amazing!

00:09:47.322 --> 00:09:49.215
You are incredible!

00:09:49.215 --> 00:09:53.659
Well we love what you are
doing and we are not alone.

00:09:53.659 --> 00:09:57.360
Comfort Keepers, a leading
in-home care provider for adults

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.060
and seniors is dedicated to
uplifting the human spirit

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.885
by delivering customized
services to meet the unique needs

00:10:02.885 --> 00:10:04.352
of your loved ones.

00:10:04.352 --> 00:10:07.530
To help you give more
caregivers a day off,

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:10.152
they're donating $10,000
just to help you out.

00:10:10.152 --> 00:10:14.354
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:10:14.354 --> 00:10:15.363
Oh my!

00:10:15.363 --> 00:10:17.936
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:10:17.936 --> 00:10:20.436
- Thank you so much!
- Hold on.

00:10:22.682 --> 00:10:25.015
You have such a great heart.

00:10:25.903 --> 00:10:27.810
And you're doing amazing things.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:29.880
And also this just brings
awareness to people

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.130
like you never know what people
are going through at home,

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.340
like what they're having to
